<p class="Heading3">Ja 113 The Story about the Deceitful Jackal<br>(Siṅgālajātaka)</p>

<p class="summary">In the present Devadatta boasts that the truth lies only with himself, and not with the Buddha. The latter tells how, in a past life, Devadatta had fooled and humiliated a brahmin who gave him help (<a href="../Jataka/113.htm">full story</a>).</p>

<p class="verse3">1. Saddahāsi siṅgālassa surāpītassa, brāhmaṇa,<br>
Sippikānaṁ sataṁ natthi, kuto kaṁsasatā duve ti.</p>

<p class="translation">You place your trust in this drunken jackal, brahmin, there is not one hundred cowries, how two hundred bronze coins?</p>

<p>In this connection, <i>there is not one hundred cowries</i> means there is indeed not even one hundred cowries. <i>How two hundred bronze coins</i> means but how could there be two hundred coins for him?</p>

<p>&#160;</p>

